<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Caribbean Community</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1973">1973</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as CARICOM</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> Community, CARICOM, is an organisation of Caribbean states and dependencies founded by the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaguaramas">Chaguaramas</a> in 1973 to promote economic integration and cooperation in foreign policy, health and education. It succeeded the Caribbean Free Trade Association and revived the regional cooperation lost with the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-indies">West Indies</a> Federation in 1962. Its secretariat is in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgetown-guyana">Georgetown, Guyana</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/caribbean-community.jpg" alt="Caribbean Community" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CARICOM · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_CARICOM.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Caribbean Community was first called the Caribbean Community and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-economic-community">Common Market</a>. It was established by the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaguaramas">Chaguaramas</a>, signed by the leaders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barbados">Barbados</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guyana">Guyana</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinidad-and-tobago">Trinidad and Tobago</a> and in effect from 1 August 1973. It superseded the Caribbean Free Trade Association of 1965-1972, which had kept up an economic link between the English-speaking countries of the Caribbean after the West Indies Federation dissolved. That federation had lasted from 3 January 1958 to 31 May 1962.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>CARICOM began as an organisation of the English-speaking Caribbean, and English was its sole working language into the 1990s. It became multilingual as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suriname">Suriname</a> joined in 1995 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a> in 2002, and Spanish was added as a fourth official language in 2003. On 5 July 2001 the heads of government signed a revised Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaguaramas">Chaguaramas</a> at Nassau, which cleared the way for the CARICOM Single Market and Economy and established the Caribbean Court of Justice. Haiti's participation was suspended from 29 February 2004 until June 2006.</p><p>CARICOM took part in founding wider bodies. The Association of Caribbean States was formed on 24 July 1994, from a recommendation of the West Indian Commission that CARICOM's heads of government set up in 1989. CARICOM was also involved in creating the Community of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin American</a> and Caribbean States in 2010.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Conference of Heads of Government and the Community Council, made up of ministers responsible for community affairs, are the two principal organs. The chairmanship rotates among the heads of government. The Secretariat in Georgetown is the chief administrative organ; its Secretary-General is the chief executive, with a five-year term that may be renewed. Its main activities are coordinating economic policy and development planning, running a regional single market, handling trade disputes and devising projects for the less developed members. Its flag, first flown on 4 July 1984, shows two interlocking Cs as broken chain links, which stand for unity and a break with the colonial past.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Caribbean Community” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Shared institutions such as the University of the West Indies and the West Indies cricket team survived, and <b>CARICOM</b> later revived regional cooperation.</blockquote><a href="/state/west-indies-federation">West Indies Federation</a></li></ul></section>
